## Case text (Ask-CK ck.db zephyr_cases, key AWPTCM-T47208)
Title: Copper pluggable with supported speeds
Objective / Precondition / Steps: (all empty in ck.db -- no step text, no expected result). Method applied (triage 2026-10-21, agreed):
on the one copper pluggable present (x230 port1.0.1, AT-SPTXa 1000BASE-T, partner AR4050S port1.0.2 fixed copper) read the module's supported
speeds, then drive the DUT through every one of them with autonegotiation (speed auto 100 / 10 / 1000), fixed 1000, and the forced
(no-autoneg) rows 100/full and 10/full with the AR4050S matched; also check that speeds the module cannot do are refused. Check = the
DUT's port status shows the expected speed/duplex, the partner's status agrees, the LLDP neighbour is present. Then restore to auto.
CAVEAT (coverage): ONE module type is on the bench (AT-SPTXa). The 2026-10-09 run also had AT-SPTX and AT-SPTXc against other partners;
those are not fitted today. Every speed the fitted module supports was exercised, so the case is PASS-able with this caveat
(logged-output.md §1: no bench gap stopped a step). Half duplex is T47210's matter and was not run here.

## Where "supported speeds" come from
1. `show system pluggable detail` (47208-u1a.out): Device Name AT-SPTXa, Device Type 1000BASE-T -> a 10/100/1000BASE-T copper SFP.
2. The x230 parser offers a generic list for `speed ?` / `speed auto ?` (10 ... 400000, 47208-qmark.out, read with tools/qmark.py, nothing executed),
   so the parser is NOT the source: the DUT accepts the three the module supports and answers
   `% port1.0.1: Unsupported speed/duplex combination port1.0.1` to `speed auto 2500`, `speed 10000` and `speed auto 100000` (47208-u1b.out).
   Supported speeds for this pluggable = 10, 100, 1000 Mb/s, by module type and by the DUT's own acceptance/refusal.

## Method / tools
tools/tb ckcon tb470 pp1323 <uN> 115200 ... with `@poll <s> <regex> do show interface portX status` from config-if (1.2 s per read);
tools/tb qmark for the `?` help probe. Stdouts kept by the tester and copied verbatim below: 47208-u1a (DUT setup reads + HEALTH BEFORE),
47208-qmark, 47208-u1b (DUT matrix), 47208-u0b (4050 polls; see the note), 47208-u0c (4050 forced 100/full), 47208-u1c (DUT verify 100/full,
then speed 10), 47208-u0d (4050 forced 10/full), 47208-u1d (DUT verify 10/full, restore to auto), 47208-u0e (4050 restore), 47208-u1e
(DUT auto 100), 47208-u0f (4050 reads a-100), 47208-u1f (DUT auto 10), 47208-u0g (4050 reads a-10), 47208-u1g (DUT restore, final reads,
HEALTH AFTER, running-config, show boot), 47208-u0h (4050 running-config, show boot, reboot history, exception log).
HH:MM:SS >>> stamps are the box's clock (NZDT); DUT log lines are UTC. Setup: none beyond the baseline (both ports at auto). .cfg files:
swi_f.cfg from 47208-u1a.out (14:00:5x, before step 1), swi_e.cfg from base-u0.out (13:48:26, unchanged baseline; the 4050 was at auto).
Note on 47208-u0b.out: it was meant to watch the 4050 during the DUT matrix, but the two tool calls ran one after the other (u0's first
read is stamped 14:04:18, after the DUT matrix ended at 14:04:1x), so its first three polls timed out with the link already at the forced
100/full row (the 4050, still at auto, read `a-half a-100` = parallel detection). The partner readings for the auto rows were therefore taken
again, sequentially, in 47208-u1e/u0f/u1f/u0g. Nothing in that file is a device fault.

